# Migration guardrails

Full context in `backend/docs/MIGRATION_PLAN.md`. These three constraints outrank convenience,
schedule, and elegance. If a change cannot satisfy all three, stop and escalate to the Management
Agent rather than working around them.

## 1. Never break a working scenario for an existing user

43 production accounts and a live medical study depend on the current path. Every change is
additive until proven otherwise.

- Firebase Auth stays the identity provider. Never migrate, rewrite, or re-key a credential.
- Never renumber a `participant_code`. All historical ID forms stay in `legacy_file_user_ids`.
- Never invalidate a signed-in session, force a re-login, or trigger a re-consent.
- A failure in the new backend must never make a legacy upload fail, retry, or be deleted early.
- Ambiguous data fails loudly (`status = 'needs_id_resolution'`) instead of guessing.

## 2. Both modes run in parallel until the tests pass

`BOTH_ARCH=true` is the default and stays the default. The legacy CSV + Google Drive + Apps Script
pipeline is authoritative; PostgreSQL runs alongside it and is reconciled nightly.

- Clients dual-write with independent markers: `.uploaded` (legacy) and `.uploaded_v2` (backend).
  On-device retention deletion keys off `.uploaded` only.
- While `BOTH_ARCH=true`, legacy Drive wins any conflict.
- Nothing on Drive is ever mutated or deleted, in any mode.
- Flip to `BOTH_ARCH=false` only after 14 consecutive clean reconciliation runs, and keep the flip
  reversible for a full phase afterwards.

## 3. Production migrates its own data on first run

Production must come up with the historical corpus already loaded, with no manual laptop step and
no one connecting to the database by hand.

- Every importer is idempotent and resumable. Running the whole thing twice changes nothing.
- Completed steps are recorded, so a crash resumes at the failed step instead of replaying.
- Take a `pg_advisory_lock` so two instances booting together cannot both migrate.
- A step that cannot finish cleanly rolls back and aborts the run. Half-imported is worse than
  not migrated.
- Every run ends by reconciling counts against the source and exits non-zero on any mismatch.
